               "cons":  [
                            "New bonds pay 7.0–8.65% interest, which is expensive money for a company that wants to be a hyperscaler.",
                            "About $260–288B of data-center leases sit off the balance sheet and run 15–19 years.",
                            "Free cash flow is negative, with capex of $28.5B in Q1, and Oracle does not expect it to turn positive before the second half of FY28.",
                            "Much of the backlog depends on one customer, OpenAI, so any slowdown there would hit Oracle directly."
                        ],
               "opportunityCheck":  "Most of the sub-scores fit what I found. The one I would move is position, down by 1–2 points. Oracle is winning large AI contracts, but it pays 7–8.65% to borrow while Microsoft, Google and Amazon fund their buildouts from cash flow, and a large share of its backlog depends on OpenAI. That is a weaker position than the backlog size suggests. AI exposure could arguably go up a point, because about 90% of RPO is cloud infrastructure. The bottleneck score of 11 already looks appropriately modest.",
               "technical":  "The trend is clearly down. At $101.19, the price is below the 50-day EMA ($129.77), the 100-day ($139.53) and the 200-day ($153.67). EMAs are exponential moving averages, which weight recent prices more. They are also stacked bearishly, with the shortest on the bottom, which is a textbook downtrend. MACD is -6.27, below its signal line at -4.82, with a histogram of -1.45. MACD is a trend-momentum gauge, and a negative histogram means selling pressure is still building. RSI 14 is 31.83. RSI is a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 counts as oversold, so the stock is close to oversold but not there yet. The price closed below the lower Bollinger Band at $103.16, the band that marks the edge of normal 20-day volatility. That shows stretched selling and often comes before a short bounce, but it does not confirm a turn. The day\u0027s low of $100.25 is also the 52-week low, and the $100 area is the nearest support. Old support near $115–116, which was the 17 Nov close and the offering price, is now overhead resistance. Volume was 47.3M shares, about 12% above the 20-day average of 42.1M, so the drop came on real selling, not thin trading.",
               "fundamental":  "Oracle combines a sticky database and applications business with a fast-growing AI cloud. In fiscal Q1 FY27 (quarter ended 31 Aug, reported 10 Sep 2026), revenue was $19.3B, up about 30%. OCI revenue rose 121% to $7.4B, RPO reached $664B and FY27 guidance was raised to at least $90B. The cost of that growth is high. Capex was $28.5B in the quarter and free cash flow was negative. At its 28 Oct Investor Day, Oracle kept its FY30 targets of $225B+ revenue and $21+ EPS. It capped new capital raises at $20B, then used all of that on 2 Nov. Next comes the fiscal Q2 report, due around mid-December 2026. Investors will look for proof that capex is turning into revenue and that the cash burn is slowing.",
               "news":  [
                            "28 Oct 2026: At Investor Day, Oracle reaffirmed its FY30 goals of $225B+ revenue, a 40%+ margin and $21+ EPS. It capped new financing at $20B, split evenly between equity and debt, and targeted positive free cash flow in the second half of FY28. The shares jumped about 11% that day, then gave back the gain by 30 Oct as chip stocks sold off.",
                            "2 Nov 2026: Oracle priced 86.2M shares at $116, an 11.2% discount to the previous close, plus $10B of notes due 2029–2046 with coupons of 7.000–8.650%. Both deals closed on 4 Nov. On the same day, Stephens upgraded the stock to Overweight with a $213 target.",
                            "10–13 Nov 2026: Bernstein kept its Outperform rating and $325 target and estimated a value of about $133 a share even if the AI business failed. DBS cut its target to $290 from $310, and President Capital trimmed its target to $323.",
                            "20 Nov 2026: Oracle said its OpenAI contract, which covers 4.5 GW of extra US capacity, is fully funded through the November raise plus vendor financing. It did not say how large the vendor financing is or who the vendors are. The stock still fell 8.48% to $101.19, 12.8% below the offering price, as AI chip stocks sold off."
                        ],
               "caution":  "This is a balance-sheet story more than a demand story. Oracle has now used its whole self-imposed $20B financing cap, so any new raise before FY30 would hurt management\u0027s credibility. The new bonds pay up to 8.65%, a sign that lenders see real risk. About $260–288B of long-term leases are locked in whatever happens to AI demand. If OpenAI slows its spending, both the backlog and the lease burden become a problem. Technically, a break below the $100 area would leave no recent support underneath.",
               "opportunity":  "Expectations are now much lower. The stock trades below the $116 offering price and near Bernstein\u0027s $133 estimate of what the non-AI business alone is worth. If fiscal Q2 results in mid-December show OCI growth staying above 100% and capex starting to turn into revenue, the \u0027funding crisis\u0027 story could fade quickly. RSI is near 30 and the price is below the lower Bollinger Band, so the selling looks stretched in the short term. A long-term holder gets a 121%-growth AI cloud with the database franchise underneath, at a much lower price.",
